if(KcancelSim==1){

    cout<<"              ---                  "<<endl;
    cout<<"Solver failed to converge within the specified tolerances. Here are some suggestions:"<<endl;
    cout<<" "<<endl;
    cout<<"1) Try lowering dtmin."<<endl;
    cout<<" "<<endl;
    cout<<"2) If m=0, check that the initial guesses for phi and VOLT are reasonable. It may require some trial and error."<<endl;
    //cout<<"3) If convergence failure occurs when approaching surface concentration depletion of reactants along the electrodes, it is likely caused by the singul"
    cout<<" "<<endl;
    cout<<"3) Try loosening the tolerance on itot."<<endl;
    cout<<" "<<endl;
    cout<<"4) When reactants along the electrodes approach depletion, a singularity will begin to form in the solution which can cause the solver to fail, particularly if the timestep is too large."<<endl;
    cout<<"In this case, the user could try: 1) Lowering dtmin, 2) Shorten the discharge period, 3) Adjust solver tolerances; stricter dt selection will enable better resolution of singularities during deep discharge"<<endl;
    cout<<" "<<endl;
    cout<<"5) A singularity in the solution will form when reacting ion concentrations begin to deplete next to the electrodes. This will occur if the cycle time is particularly long or if"<<endl;
    cout<<"the applied current is very high."<<endl;
    cout<<" "<<endl;
    cout<<"6) If using a step-like applied current, make sure the transitions in current are not too sharp. If solver fails at the point of current change, try making the step-function smoother."<<endl;
    cout<<" "<<endl;
    cout<<"7) Check breakpoint file. If the breakpoints are wrong, the solver can use a timestep that's too large in places where Iapp exhibits high curvature."<<endl;
    cout<<"              ---                  "<<endl;

}


if(KcancelSim==0){

    cout<<"              ---                  "<<endl;
    cout<<"--- Simulation reached the end --- "<<endl;
    cout<<"              ---                  "<<endl;



}



